\id EZR Original Douay-Rheims (1582/1610)
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h 1 Esdras
\toc1 The First Book of Esdras.
\toc2 1 Esdras
\toc3 EZR
\mt1 The First Book of Esdras.
\is THE ARGVMENT OF THE BOOKES OF ESDRAS.
\ip \it ESDRAS\it* a holie Prieſt and Scribe, of the ſtocke of Aaron, by the line of Eleazar, writeth the \f - \ft The two Bookes of Eſdras and Nehemias are but one in the Hebrew.\f* \f - \ft \+it 1. Eſdr. 7.\+it*\f* hiſtorie of God's people, in, and preſently after their captiuitie in Babilon: which \it Nehemias\it* another godlie Prieſt proſecuteth, whoſe Booke is alſo called the ſecond of Eſdras, becauſe in the Hebrew and Greke they are but one Booke, relating the acts of them both. The other two books called \it the third and fourth\it* of Eſdras, touching the ſame matter, \it are\it* not in the Hebrew, nor \it receiued into\it* \f - \ft The third and fourth are not canonical.\f* \it the Canon of holie Scripture\it*, though the Greke Church hold the third booke as Canonicall, and placeth it firſt, becauſe it conteyneth thinges donne before the other.
\ip In the two here folowing, which are vndoubtedly holie Scripture S. Ierom ſayth, that \f - \ft This hiſtorie hath alſo a ſpiritual ſenſe.\f* \f - \ft \+it Epiſt. ad Paulin.\+it*\f* \it Eſdras and Nehemias (to witte the Helper, & Comforter from God) reſtored the Temple, and built the walles of the citie\it*; adding that \it al the troope of the people returning into their countrie, alſo the deſcription of Prieſtes, Leuites, Iſraelites, Proſelites, and the workes of walles and towres diuided by ſeueral families\it*, aliud in cortice præferunt, aliud in medulla retinent, \it shew one thing in the barke, kepe another thing in the marrow\it*: ſignifying that this hiſtorie hath both a literal, and a myſtical ſenſe. According to the letter, this firſt Booke sheweth the reduction of Gods people from \f - \ft Firſt booke diuided into two partes.\f* Babylon; In the firſt ſix chapters. In the other foure, their inſtruction by Eſdras after their returne.
\is THE CONTINUANCE OF THE CHURCH AND RELIGION, IN THE FIFTH AGE. From the foundation of the Temple, to the captivity in Babylon. The ſpace of 430. years.
\ip \it ALBEIT\it* there were greater Schiſms, Hereſies, and more revolts from God's law and ſervice in this fifth age, than in the former: Yet the true Church and Religion continued ſtill, and were no leſs \f - \ft The Church ſtill viſible, and the ſame faith as before.\f* conſpicuous than before. Which being clear and evident, touching many principal Articles, Wwe will here only remitte the reader to ſome ſpecial places, for confirmation thereof: neither will we be prolixe in declaring other points denied, or called into controverſy at this time, by the impugners of Catholic Religion.
\ip \it Belief in one God\it* appeareth plainly in building, adorning, and dedicating the Temple with ſo \f - \ft One God.\f* great ſolemnity of the Prieſts, Levites, and all the Tribes, and particularly by King Salomon's prayer, 3. Reg. 7. & 8. 2. Paral. 2. &c. Alſo Proverb. 8. Eccle. 12. Iſaie 41. 44. 45. The Myſtery of \f - \ft Three Perſons.\f* the \it B. Trinity\it*, Prover. 12. Iſaie 6. 48. 49. Oſe. 11. Ioel. 2. \it Of Chriſt our Redeemer\it*, Iſaie. 7. 8. 9. 11. \f - \ft Chriſt.\f* 28. 53. Ierem. 23. 30. 33. Ezech. 17. 34. 37. Dan. 7. 9. Oſee. 6. 11. 14. Ioel. 2. Sophon. 2. Aggai 2. Zachar. 2. &c. \it Sacrifices, Sacraments\it*, & other Rites the ſame as before. But more frequent \f - \ft Sacrifices.\f* Prophecies, that they \it should be changed into better\it* and perfecter \it by Chriſt\it*. Prov. 9. Iſai. 12. 52. \f - \ft Sacraments to be changed by Chriſt.\f* 55. 61. In the meane time for more ſignification of the \it ſingular virtue of Chriſt's Sacraments\it*, the effect of \it penitential works\it* is often recorded. For example, wicked \it Achab by hair-cloth, faſting, and\it* other \it humiliation eſcaped\it* part of his deſerued \it punishment\it*, 3. Reg. 21. \it Manaſſes recovered God's\it* \f - \ft Fruits of penance.\f* \it fauoure\it*, and \it his temporal Kingdom\it*, 2. Par. 33. Who yet was punished in his poſterity. 4. Reg. 23. And the \it Ninivites\it* by ſuch penance \it auoided deſtruction\it*. Ionae 3. Yea nothing is more frequent in the Prophets than preaching of penance. Iſa. 1. 2. 3. 30. Iere. 3. 18. &c. and others, all aſcribing the cauſe of plagues, and afflictions to the want of repentance. And \it falſe Prophets\it* condemned of error and falſe doctrine, \it for promiſing the people peace\it*, and ſecurity \it in their ſins\it*. Ierem. 14. Lamen. 2. Beſides \it abſtinence\it* from diverſe ſorts of meats, counted unclean (Iſaiae 66.) and \it ordinarie faſts\it*, \f - \ft Abſtinence.\f* according to the law, \it other faſts\it* were \it appointed\it* ſometimes, vpon occaſions requiring, not \f - \ft Faſts.\f* only to ſubdue, and mortify the flesh, but alſo \it to obtaine mercy\it* at God's hands in ſpecial diſtreſſes. 2. Par. 20. Ioel. 1. 2. Ionae 3. \it Elias faſting forty days\it*, 3. Reg. 19. prefigured \it Chriſt's faſt\it*. Which the Church imitateth in \it Lent of forty days\it*, according to human hability, for the faſts of Chriſt, \f - \ft Lent.\f* Elias, and Moyſes were miraculous.
\ip To the Feaſts inſtituted before, was added the \it Dedication of the Temple\it*. 3. Reg. 7. 2. Par. 3. \f - \ft Feaſts.\f*
\ip \it Which was built in Mount Moria\it*, 2. Par. 3. the place \it deſigned long before\it* for this purpoſe, when \f - \ft Place of the Temple deſigned long before.\f* Abraham was directed thither by God, and was there ready to ſacrifice his ſon Iſaac. Gen. 22.
\ip Where Dauid alſo offered ſacrifice. 2. Reg. 24, 1. Par. 21.
\ip This being the \it only ordinarie place for Sacrifice\it*, there were for other uſes of daylie prayer, reading, preaching, and hearing the word of God other \it Synagogues built\it* (as it were Parish churches) in \f - \ft Synagogues.\f* great number: in Jeruſalem it-ſelf foure hundred and four ſcore, and many more in the whole Kingdom, as the Hebrew Traditions teſtify. Of all which places, eſpecially \it of the Temple\it*, there \f - \ft Sanctuary.\f* was \it venerable reſpect\it* had. For which cauſe when Joiada the High Prieſt gaue order to kil A thalia, he ſuffered it not to be donne in the Temple, but commanded firſt to draw her forth. 4. Reg. 11. 2.
\ip Par. 23. And as peculiar places, ſo \it ſpecial Pſalms\it*, and \it Hymns\it* were \it appointed\it* for diverſe \f - \ft Set form of prayers.\f* purpoſes and occaſions. 2. Par. 20.
\ip The \it miniſtry of Angels\it* was very Vſual in this time. One was \it ſent to\it* comfort and direct \it Elias\it* the \f - \ft Miniſtry of Angels.\f* prophet in his afflictions. 3. Reg. 19. 4. Reg. 1. \it An Angel ſtruck the Aſſyrians' whole campe\it*, 4. Reg. 19. 2. Par. 32. Alſo the \it Interceſſion of Angels is\it* ſo evident, Tobiae 12. Raphael offering Tobias' prayer to God \it , that Proteſtants have no other refuge to avoid this point of faith, but by denying the Booke to be Canonical Scripture\it*.
\ip \it Honour of\it* other \it Saints, and\it* their \it Interceſſion\it* is proved \it a Maiori\it*. For ſo much as honour was \f - \ft Honour and Interceſſion of Saints.\f* religiouſly exhibited to ſpiritual power and excellency, in men yet living in this world. So \it a Noble man adored Elias\it* the Prophet, being farre greater than he in ciuil and Wworldlie reſpects. 3. Reg. 18.
\ip \it Eliſeus\it* alſo was \it adored by his diſciples\it*, not for anie worldly authority or eminence, but \it for his ſpiritual power and ſuperiority\it* amongſt them. 4. Reg. 2. Likewiſe all \it Prophets\it* and \it Prieſts\it* were \it religiouſly honoured for their holie and ſpiritual functions\it*. 3. Reg. 13. Much more Saints are rightly honoured, being immortal and in eternal glory. It appeareth alſo that \it Elias ſeven\it* years \it after\it* that \it he was tranſlated\it* from human converſation (when Eliſeus was chiefe Prophet, 4. Reg. 3. which was in or before the eighteenth yeare of Ioſaphat, who reigned five and twenty, 3. Reg. 22.) \it had care of Joram\it*, and his kingdom, \it admonishing him by letters\it* of God's wrath, againſt him and his people for their ſins. 2. Par. 21. And the Scripture ſaith often, that \it God ſpared and protected Jeruſalem, and the Kingdom of Juda for Dauids ſake\it*. 3. Reg. 11. 15. 4. Reg. 8. 19. 20. 2. Par. 6. 21. Iſa. 37. We have \f - \ft Relics.\f* alſo example of \it Saints' Relics\it* in the \it cloak of Elias.\it* 4. Reg. 2. in \it Eliſeus' bones\it*, 4. Reg. 13. and in \it another Prophet's body buried\it* in Bethel. Which Ioſias would not ſuffer to be touched. 4. Reg. 23.
\ip \it Images\it* were conſerved \it in the Temple\it*, 3. Reg. 7. as before in the Tabernacle: when idolatry was \f - \ft Images.\f* moſt deſtroyed. 3. Reg. 15. 4. Reg. 23. yea an abuſe riſing of the \it brazen ſerpent\it*, for which Ezechias \it deſtroyed it\it*, 4. Reg. 18. yet he touched not the Images of Cherubins in the Temple. Which none but Infidels ſought to deſtroy. And \it Oſee\it* the Prophet (ch. 3.) \it bewayleth the want of Theraphim\it* or \it Images\it*, amongſt other ſacred things, Sacrifice, Altar, and Ephod. Whereby the ancient Rabbins prove very Wel, that \it Images of Angels\it* (and the ſame of other Saints) are \it not contrary to the Decalogue\it*, but the images of Idols. \it Good works\it* were \it rewarded\it*, and \it bad punished\it*, 3. Reg. 9. and the whole hiſtory \f - \ft Good works meritorious.\f* of this age teſtifieth the ſame. Where by the way may be obſerued, that ſome \it juſt men fell from their iuſtice\it*, as Salomon 1. Par. 28. 3. Reg. 11. Joas, 4. Reg. 12. 2. Par. 24. Ozias, 2. Paral. 26. \it Others from wickedneſs returned to piety\it*, as Manaſſes 4. Reg. 23. 2. Par. 33. the multitude of the people very often much following the diſpoſition of their Kings. \it Special State of life\it* not commanded \f - \ft Evangelical counſels prefigured.\f* by the law, was \it voluntarily profeſſed\it*, and obſerved by ſome Prophets, and their diſciples called \it the children of Prophets\it*: Keeping \it particular Rules\it*, and wearing \it diſtinct habit\it*, 4. Reg. 1. 2. 4. The orders of \it Nazarites\it*, and \it Rechabites\it* inſtituted before, \it continued\it* ſtill, Amos. 2. Ierem. 35. All which \f - \ft Chaſtity of clergy men, & religious orders.\f* were very exemplar \it figures of Religious State, and Orders in the new Teſtament\it*, and perpetual chaſtity of clergie men embraced by ſuch as folow \it Evangelical counſels\it*, propoſed, and not commanded by our Sauiour. To which S. Paul likewiſe exhorteth, though there be no precept \f - \ft \+it Mat. 19. 1. Cor. 7. Act. 5. 1. Tim. 5.\+it*\f* thereof to any, before they bind themſelves.
\ip \it Exequies for the dead\it* were continually kept, as the ſacred hiſtory witneſſeth, recording where \f - \ft Solemn Exequies for the dead.\f* and with what ſolemnity the Kings were buried. Which would be overlong, and needleſs to recite: the like is alſo Writen of ſome Prophets. 3. Reg. 13. 4. Reg. 23. Holie \it Tobias\it* by example and fatherlie admonition \it exhorted\it* his ſon \it to do works of mercy\it*, not only to the living, but alſo \it to the dead. Put thy bread and thy wine vpon the ſepulture of the juſt\it*. ch. 4. Iſaias, ch. 57. as the Jews both underſtood and practiſed, \it prayed, that peace be given to the juſt, in his couch\it*, or reſting place after his death. \it Of the general Reſurrection\it*, Elias' tranſlation is a figure, who yet living sheweth, that \f - \ft \+it Gen. 5.\+it*\f* God can and will reſtore all men to life again in their bodies, after death, as he conſerveth him, an Enoch in their mortal bodies without corruption. Ezechiel alſo \it propheſieth of the Reſurrection\it* \f - \ft Reſurrection.\f* of the dead, applying it myſtically to ſpiritual reſurrection, and reſtoration of Iſrael to former ſtate. ch. 37. Of the \it laſt Judgement\it*, and \it eternal glory to\it* the good, and everlaſting \it pain\it* to the \f - \ft Judgement.\f* wicked, Salomon agreeably to the doctrine of other Prophets, diſcourſeth in his booke of \f - \ft Eternal glory or pain.\f* Eccleſiaſtes, namely ch. 3. 11. and in the laſt concludeth thus: \it Let vs altogether heare the end of ſpeaking: Feare God, and obſerue his commandments: for this is every man\it* (or, to this end man is created) \it and God will bring into Judgement all things, that are done, for every error\it* (or obſcure thing) \it whether it be good or evil\it*.
\ip Neither were theſe and other \it points of Faith and Religion\it* interrupted, but \it ſtill believed and\it* \f - \ft Church without interruption.\f* \it profeſſed\it* in the \it Church always viſible and incontaminate\it*, notwithſtanding \it ſome boughs\it* and branches became unfruitful, and rotten: others \it brake off and were ſeparated\it* from this vine. For when \it Salomon falling\it* to luxury, multiplying manie wives and concubines, was by them ſeduced and brought \it to ſpiritual fornication\it* and idolatry, \it making altars, and offering ſacrifices to Idols, the Prieſts, Prophets, and people generally perſeuered in God's law\it* and ſervice. 3. Reg. 11. After \f - \ft Jeroboam's wicked policy.\f* whoſe death \it Jeroboam\it* his ſeruant, of the tribe of Ephraim, \it poſſeſſing Tenne Tribes\it* (called the Kingdom of Iſrael) to maintain his new ſtate, fearing that if the people reſorted to Jeruſalem for religion's ſake, they would depart from him, and returne to the right heirs of Dauid and Salomon, \it made an egregious Schiſm, ſetting up two golden calves in Bethel and Dan\it*, 3. Reg. 12. \it made temples, altars, and Prieſts\it* to ſerve them, all oppoſite to God's ordinance. But not only the other \it Two Tribes\it*, called the Kingdom of Juda, but alſo \it the greateſt part of Iſrael\it*, eſpecially \it Prieſts, Leuites\it*, and \it devouteſt people, repayred ſtill to Jeruſalem, not yelding to\it* that \it ſchiſm\it* and idolatry. 2. Par. 11. Moreover God raiſed up and \it ſent\it* ſpecial \it Prophets\it* to confirm the Wweake and recall the ſeduced.
\ip For Jeroboam has no ſooner ſet up his new altar in Bethel, and begunne to offer incenſe upon it, \f - \ft Prophets inſpired by God to reſiſt Schiſm and Hereſy.\f* but \it a Prophet came out of Juda in the word of our Lord\it*, and cried againſt that altar, foretelling that whereas for that preſent they burnt frankincenſe upon it, the time should come, when the falſe Prieſts should be burned there, confirming by preſent \it miracles\it* that which he auerred in words, \f - \ft \+it 4. Reg. 23.\+it*\f* \it the King's hand ſuddenly withering\it*, and \it reſtored\it* again \it by the Prophet's prayer\it*, and the new \it altar cleaving\it* in ſunder, that the ashes fell out. 3. Reg. 13. Further another Prophet called \it Ahias\it* \f - \ft The often change of Kings, and evil ſucceed in the Kingdom of Iſrael.\f* \it foreshewed\it* the deſtruction and utter \it extirpation of Ieroboams family\it*, for his enormous Wwickednes, and namely (which is moſt often inculcate) \it for making Iſrael to ſin\it*, by deuiſing and ſetting abroach a new religion. 3. Reg. 14. which ruin happened very shortly. For himſelf reigning twenty two \f - \ft The firſt family reigned but 24. years.\f* years (3. Reg. 14.) one of his ſons died preſently \it according to the Prophet's word\it*. v. 18. Another called \it Nadab\it* ſucceeding to his father, reigned only two year, and was \it ſlain\it* together \it with their whole race\it* and kindred, by \it Baaſa\it* of the tribe of Iſſachar. 3. Reg. 15. Likewiſe Baaſa folowing \f - \ft The ſecond new family. 26.\f* the bad ſteps of Jeroboam was forewarned by \it Jehu a Prophet\it*, that his houſe should alſo be deſtroyed. And accordingly when he had reigned foure and twenty years, his ſon \it Ela\it* reigned but two years, was \it ſlain\it* by his ſervant \it Zambri\it*, and all his kindred deſtroyed. Which Zambri \f - \ft The third but 7. days.\f* reigned but ſeven days. For being forthwith beſieged by \it Amri\it*, of the tribe of Beniamin, he deſperately burned himſelf together with the King's palace. Neither did Amri then poſſeſs the \f - \ft The fourth, 48.\f* Kingdome with peace. For he being choſen King by the armie only, whereof he was General, another part of the people choſe & followed \it Thebni. Whereof\it* aroſe \it ciuil War\it* between the Anti-Kings, continuing three years: til Thebni died, and ſo Amri reigned alone, but wickedly as his predeceſſors, twelve years in all. Then ſucceeded his ſon \it Achab\it* moſt wicked. \it Who maried Jezabel\it* a Sidonian, & by her was perſuaded to worship Baal. 3. Reg. 16. To him notwithſtanding God ſent manie admonitions by ſundry Prophets, and beſtowed great benefits upon him, whereupon he did ſome notorious penitential works, but not perſevering in any good thing, returned to his wickedneſs. 3. Reg. 20. And finally \it believing falſe Prophets\it*, and perſecuting Micheas for propheſying the truth, was ſlain in battle when he thought himſelf moſt ſecure. 3. Reg. 22. having reigned twentie two years. 3. Reg. 16. His ſon \it Ochozias\it* reigning but two years fell through a window, and died of the hurt. 4. Reg. 1. His other ſon Joram, after twelue years was \it ſlain\it* by \it Jehu\it* of another family: who then diſpatched \it Jezabel\it*, and leaving her in the ſtreet, the dogs did eat \f - \ft The fifth. 103.\f* her carcaſs. He alſo cauſed ſeventy ſons of Joram to be ſlain, and vtterly deſtroyed all Achab's houſe. 4. Reg. 10. For which ſervice he was eſtablished in the Kingdom, for foure generations, v. 30. So himſelf reigning twentie eight years, 3. Reg. 10. after him reigned ſucceſſively his ſon \it Joachaz\it* ſeventeen years, his ſon \it Joas\it* ſixtene years, 4. Reg. 13. his ſon \it Jeroboam\it* one and forty years. Laſtly his ſon \it Zacharias\it*, whom his ſeruant \it Sellum\it*, of another race, killed when he had reigned but ſix months, 4. Reg. 15. And after one month \it Sellum\it* was ſlain by \it Manahen\it* of another progenie. \f - \ft The ſixth, one month.\f*
\ip Who reigned tenne years. Then his ſon \it Phaceia\it* reigning two years, was ſlain by \it Phacee\it* of \f - \ft The ſeventh, 12. years.\f* another generation. He reigning twenty years, \it many\it* of his people were \it carried captive into\it* \f - \ft The eighth 20. years.\f* \it Aſſiria\it*, and himſelf was ſlain by \it Oſee\it* of another kindred. 4. Reg. 15. Finally \it the Aſſyrians\it* \f - \ft The ninth nine years.\f* taking Samaria by three years ſiege, in the ninth yeare of Oſee \it poſſeſſed the Kingdome of Iſrael, and led all the principal perſons captives\it* into Aſſyria: about two hundred forty two years \f - \ft Then overthrown, and the Kingdom never reſtored.\f* after that Jeroboam firſt reigned over the Ten Tribes. Thus there were in all \it nineteen Kings. Beſides Thebni\it*, who only reigned in part againſt another. Of which the firſt Jeroboam, and Jehu were advanced by God's ordinance, for punishment of others. Amri was choſen by the army, the reſt of the people chooſing Thebni. Six invaded by mere force, killing their predeceſſors. The reſt ſucceeded, by ſuch titles as their fathers had. And though ſome were better ſome worſe than others, all were wicked, and at laſt overthrown.
\ip Contrarywiſe \it in the Kingdome of Juda\it* ſtanding after the ſeparation of tenne tribes about foure \f - \ft The Kingdom of Juda for David's ſake conſerved in his ſeed.\f* hundred years, though ſome Kings were wicked, yet ſome were good, and in them all \it God preſerved Dauids ſeed, by the line of Salomon\it*, in this direct ſucceſſion: \it Roboam, Abias, Aſa, Ioſaphat, Joram, Ochozias, Ioas\it* (in whoſe infancy, his grandmother \it A thalia uſurped\it* the Kingdome ſix years) \it Amaſias, Ozias, Joathan, Achaz, Ezechias, Manaſſes, Amon, Joſias, Joachaz\it* (hitherto the ſon ever ſucceeding his father) then \it Ioakim\it* (brother of Joachaz) Ioachin otherwiſe called \it Jechonias\it*, ſon of Joachaz. And finally his Uncle \it Sedecias\it*, who was carried captive into Babylon. But \it Jechonias by God's ſpecial providence\it*, was \it favoured & exalted\it* by a new King of Babilon, whither he was led captive before. \it In whoſe line David's offſpring continued\it* though not with title of Kings, \it yet in eminent ſtate\it*, and eſtimation. As reſteth to be noted in the ſixth age of the world.
\ip The \it progeny\it* alſo \it of Aaron continued\it* in their office and function of Prieſthood, \it with ſucceſſion of\it* \f - \ft Succeſſion of Prieſts continued.\f* \it High Prieſts\it*, as before from Aaron to Sadoc, partly in the line of Eleazar, partly of Ithamar, both Aaron's ſons, ſo from Sadoc, by the like ſucceſſion of both families. For \it of Eleazar\it* is recorded this \it Genealogie\it* 1. Paral. 6. \it Sadoc, Achimaas, Azarias, Iohanan, Azarias, Amarias, Achitob, Sadoc, Sellum, Helcias, Azarias, Zaraias\it*, and \it Joſedech\it*, who was High Prieſt in the captivity (v. 15.) being caried into Babilon in the firſt tranſmigration with King Jechonias, before the general captivity of all, as it ſeemeth 4. Reg. 24. his father \it Zaraias\it* yet living, who was \it ſlain\it* nine years after by Nabuchodonoſor, 4. Reg. 25. And amongſt theſe there were ſome \it High Prieſts of Ithamar's line\it*. To wit, \it Joram, Ioiada\it*, (4. Reg. 11. 2. Par. 23.) \it Joathan, Vrias\it*, (4. Reg. 16.) and ſome others; or els ſome of the above mentioned, had other names, recited by Ioſephus lib. 10. cap. 11. Antiq. and Nicephorus lib. 2. cap. 4. Hiſt. Eccles.
\ip Moreover beſides this ordinarie ſucceſſion of Prieſts, there was an \it extraordinary miſſion of\it* \f - \ft Extraordinary miſſion of Prophets.\f* \it Prophets\it*: to ſupply more fullie the office of preaching the truth, and admonishing offenders. And theſe God \it inſpired\it* and ſent, moſt eſpecially \it when and where errors ſprung, and ſins moſt abounded\it*: giving them \it extraordinarie grace\it* and moſt excellent virtues, to counterpoiſe the enormities of wicked men. Such were in the times of Achab and Jezabel, in the kingdome of Iſrael, beſides many others, the \it two famous great Prophets Elias, & Eliſeus\it*. Whoſe admirable lives and holy \f - \ft Great effects of their preaching and miracles.\f* converſation were a mirror to the world, and great terror to the wicked, whoſe works and miracles marvelouſly confirmed the well diſpoſed, encouraged the weak, conuerted many tranſgreſſors, confounded falſe Prophets, juſtified their own preaching, and much glorified God. \it Elias 1. shut\it* \f - \ft Elias his miracles.\f* \it the heaven, that it rayned not\it* in three years. \it 2. was fedde by rauens. 3. Multiplied a poore widow's meale and oile. 4. Raiſed her dead ſon to life\it* (3. Reg. 17.) \it 5. Brought fire from heauen, to burne his ſacrifice\it*: thereby confounding four hundred and fifty falſe prophets of Baal. \it 6.\it* by prayer \it procured rain.\it* (3. Reg. 18.) \it 7. Faſted\it* without eating or drinking \it forty days\it* and nights together. (3. Reg. 19.) \it 8. Procured fire\it* from heaven, \it which deuoured two\it* inſolent \it captains, and their hundred men\it* (4. Reg. 1.) \it 9. Diuided\it* the river of \it Jordan\it* with his cloak, that himſelf and Eliſeus paſs over the dry channel. \it 10. Was aſſumpted in a fiery chariot\it* into ſome place, where he yet liveth. And parting away \it obtained\it* of God the like \it double ſpirit\it* (of prophecy and miracles) to Eliſeus. In like maner \it Eliſeus 1.\it* \f - \ft Eliſeus his miracles.\f* \it diuided Jordan againe by Elias' cloak\it*, and ſo returned to his diſciples. \it 2. Amended\it* the bitterneſs of certain \it waters, by caſting in ſalt. 3. Boys\it* being \it curſed by him\it*, for deriding him, \it were\it* forthwith torn by bears (4. Reg. 2.) \it 4.\it* He \it procured water without rain\it* for three Kings in the camp (4. Reg. 3.) \it 5. Multiplied\it* a poor widow's \it oil. 6. By his prayers a barren woman became fruitful. 7.\it* He \it raiſed\it* her ſon \it from death. 8. Made\it* the \it bitter broth\it* of his diſciples \it ſweet. 9. Fedde manie with few loaves\it* (4. Reg. 4.) \it 10. Cured Naaman of leproſy. 11. Struck Giezi with the ſame\it* (4. Reg. 5.) \it 12. Made iron to ſwim. 13. Knew the ſecret counſels\it* of the Syrian \it King. 14. Made one ſee horſe-men, and fiery chariots, which to others were inviſible. 15. Made the Syrians blinde\it*, that were ſent to apprehend him, and ſo ledde them into Samaria. \it 16. Foreshewed unexpected plentie of corn\it* the next day, with the \it death of\it* a great \it man, that would not believe it\it* (4. Reg. 7.) \it 17.\it* And after his death \it another man's dead body touching his bones was revived.\it* 4. Reg. 13.
\ip Other Prophets wrought alſo miracles, but theſe for examples may ſuffice to shew, that \it God\it* \f - \ft Religion not wholly deſtroyed in the Kingdom of Iſrael.\f* \it preſerved religion alſo in the Kingdom of Iſrael\it*. Which himſelf further teſtified, euen in moſt deſolate times, when Elias lamented, that he was left alone (3. Reg. 19.) For God anſwered, \it that ſeven thouſand\it* (meaning thereby a great multitude) \it had not bowed their knees to Baal\it*, not ſo much as in outward shevve conformed themſelues to infidelity, or idolatry. \it Jehu\it* in his time, deſtroyed all the \it worshippers of Baal.\it* (4. Reg. 10.) But \it none\it* at any time \it could wholy deſtroy true Iſraelites\it*: For \it God would not ſuffer it.\it* 4. Reg. 14. v. 27.
\ip Yea notwithſtanding \it diverſe notorious hereſies\it* were preached, & followed \it in that Kingdom of\it* \f - \ft Hereſies in the Kingdom of Iſrael.\f* \it the Tenne tribes\it*, yet all did not fall, nor embrace them. \it Jeroboam\it* not onlie made and ſet up golden calues, but alſo \it taught that\it* they were Gods, ſaying: \it Behold\it* thy Gods, O Iſrael, which \it brought\it* \f - \ft Jeroboamites.\f* \it thee out of the land of Aegypt\it*. 3. Reg. 12. \it making temples, altars\it*, and \it imaginary Prieſts, which were not of the children of Levi\it*. Alſo \it a feaſt the fifteenth day of the month, after the ſimilitude of the ſolemnity, that was celebrated in Juda\it*. All which the holie Scripture ſaith: \it He forged of his own heart\it*. That very property of Arch-heretics. But the \it true Prieſts, Leuites, &\it* many \it others, that had\it* \f - \ft Many conſtant in true religion.\f* \it given their heart to ſeek our Lord, went into Jeruſalem, to immolate theire victims before our Lord the God of their fathers\it*. 2. Par. 11. Yea \it Naaman\it* a ſtranger of Syria, and a Neophite in religion, \it taught\it* by his example, \it that none may yeld conformity, nor otherwiſe communicate with Infidels, than God's Prieſts and Prophets approue for lawful\it*. 4. Reg. 5.
\ip Unto this hereſy of Jeroboam, \it Achab by Jezabel's perſuaſion added the worshipping of Baal\it* as God, \f - \ft Jezabelites.\f* 3. Reg. 16. making both temple and altar to him in Samaria. Jeroboam's prieſts ſerving fitly this purpoſe. Though all the former heretics no more agreed to this new hereſy, than Lutherans now admitte of Calviniſm. For \it Jehu a Jeroboamite deſtroyed all Jezabelites\it* that he could by a ſtratagem get together. 4. Reg. 10. v. 28. 29. Much leſs did all Iſrael ſerve Baal.
\ip Againe, after that Salmanazar King of Aſſyria had taken Samaria, and placed there a new \f - \ft Samaritanites, divided into many Sects.\f* people, 4. Reg. 17. they learning the rites of the Iſraelites' \it religion, mixed\it* their \it Paganiſm\it* therewith, and \it made a new hereſy\it*, or rather \it many new hereſies\it*. For being \it diuers nations\it* they \it had\it* in ſeveral conventicles their \it particular gods\it*, and \it ſo many diuers Sects\it*. The \it Babylonians, Cutheites, Emathites, Heueites\it*, and \it Sapharvaimites\it*. 4. Reg. 17. But as the Prieſts, which taught them rites of true religion, allowed not of this mixture, ſo doubtleſs ſome people harkened to their admonitions, and kept religion ſimply and ſincerely. And at this very time of the Tenne tribes' captivity, \it holy Tobias\it* \f - \ft Tobias never yielded to Schiſm.\f* who was carried captive with the reſt, \it neither before nor after the captivity, left the law of God. But went to Jeruſalem\it* (when others ſerved Jeroboam's golden calves) to the Temple of our Lord, and there adored the Lord God of Iſrael. And in captivity beſtowed himſelf in works of mercie towards the living and dead of his nation. Tob. 1.
\ip As for \it the Kingdom of Juda\it*, it was \it more free from hereſies\it*. For very few or none of thoſe Kings \f - \ft The Kingdom of Juda more free from hereſy.\f* that fell to other groſs enormities, yea to manifeſt idolatry, became heretics, as is probablie collected by that Iſaias the Prophet being ſent to Achaz, admonished him, converſed and dealt with him, as with one that believed wholly and ſolidly true religion: aſſuring him that God would protect Jeruſalem, bidding him not to \it fear the two ſmoking firebrands, in the wrath of Raſin King of Syria, and of Phacce King of Iſrael\it*. Iſa. 7. Further bidding him ask a ſign of God, he anſwered, \f - \ft King Achaz.\f* though frowardly, yet not as an infidel: \it I will not ask: and I will not tempt our Lord\it*. Yea though \f - \ft Urias high Prieſt.\f* \it Vrias\it* the \it High Prieſt\it* by commandment of the ſame King (4. Reg. 16.) \it made a new altar\it* in place of God's Altar, \it yet\it* he \it erred not in faith\it*, nor in doctrine, as teaching in Moyſes chayre, \it but in fact\it* only, and of frailty for feare of the King, as the King offended in his external act, to flatter the King of Syria. And in this caſe God ſent Iſaias to admonish the King, which Urias neglected, or durſt not do. Likewiſe \it Joram\it* (4. Reg. 8. 2. Par. 21.) \it Ochozias\it* (2. Par. 22.) \it Joas\it* in the later part of his life, \f - \ft King Joram and others committing idolatry in fact, many others ſtill profeſſed true Religion.\f* 2. Par. 24. \it Manaſſes\it* in the former part of his reigne (4. Reg. 2. 2. Par. 33.) and ſome other Kings of Juda committing idolatry, and making others to fall with them, either were \it not wholly perverted\it*, or at leaſt \it drew not all with them\it*. For not only \it Prophets, in whoſe hand\it* (or miniſtry) \it God ſpake\it*, and reproved theſe ſins, but many others kept their Zeal of true religion, as appeared in their promptneſs to ſerue God, when by good Kings Aſa, Joſaphat, Ezechias, Ioſias, and others, they were exhorted, or admitted ſo to do. 4. Reg. 18. 23. 2. Par. 15. 17. 29. 30. 31. 33. 34. &c.
\ip Finally, whereas diverſe \it good Princes diſpoſed, things belonging to Diuine ſervice\it* in the temple, \it correcting faults\it*, and \it punishing offenders\it* in that behalf (3. Reg. 15. 4. Reg. 18. 23.) they \it did the ſame without prejudice of the High Prieſt's ſupremacy\it* in ſpiritual cauſes, and their godly acts \it make nothing for the English Paradox of Layheadship\it*. For ſuperior authoritie and ordinary power is \f - \ft Authority depending upon divine ordinance, is not changed by facts or practice.\f* not proved by facts good or evil, but rather by God's ordinance and inſtitution. For as the facts of uſurpers make no lawful preſcription, ſo neither the facts of good men, do change God's general ordinance and law: But are done either by way of execution, or ſometimes by diſpenſation. Often alſo by commiſſion and ſpecial inſpiration of God. As King David by diſpenſation did eat \f - \ft Good Kings defended and promoted religion not as chief in ſpiritual cauſes, but by way of execution, diſpenſation, or commiſſion. Prieſts by their negligence do ſin but loſe not their authority.\f* \f - \ft \+it Mat. 12.\+it*\f* the holy bread, which was ordained for Prieſts only. 1. Reg. 21. He diſpoſed of Prieſts' and Levites' offices about the Arke of God. Par. 15. 19. by way of execution according to the law. And of the like offices in the Temple (when it should be built) 1. Par. 23. 24. 25. 26. by divine inſpiration. And Salomon by commiſſion from God depoſed Abiathar the High Prieſt from his office, and put Sadoc in his place. 3. Reg. 2. Wherefore albeit good Kings did excellently well in calling together the Prieſts, and diſpoſing them in their offices, for execution of God's ſervice, yea in commanding what they should do 4. Reg. 18. 19. 22. and in punishing Prieſts (4. Reg. 23.) yet they did ſuch things as \it God's Commiſſioners\it*, not as ordinarie Superiors \it in ſpiritual cauſes\it*, and ſtill the ordinary ſubordination made by the law, Deut. 17. Num. 27. ſtood firme and inuiolable, the \it High Prieſt ſupreme Iudge\it* of all doubts in faith, cauſes, and quarels in religion, when other ſubordinate inferior Judges varied in their judgements. Of which offices Malachias the Prophet: (c. 2.) admonisheth Prieſts in his time, that whereas they were negligent, not performing their duty, their ſin was the greater, for that their authoritie ſtill remained, and the perpetual Rule of the law, that \it the lips of the Prieſt shall kepe knowledge, and they\it* (other men generally) \it shall require the law of his mouth, becauſe he is the Angel of the Lord of hoſts\it*. And all Princes & others \it were to receive the\it* \f - \ft \+it Deut. 17. v. 18.\+it*\f* \it law at the Prieſt's hand\it* of the Levitical Tribe. This was the warrant of ſtability in truth of the Synagogue in the old Teſtament. Much more \it the Church\it* and Spouſe \it of Chriſt\it*, whoſe \f - \ft The Church of the old Teſtament conſerved in truth. Much more the Church of Chriſt.\f* excellencie and ſingular privileges \it Salomon deſcribeth in his canticle of canticles\it*, hath ſuch \f - \ft \+it in Pſal. 30. conc. 2. li. 3. c. 32. doctrine Chriſt. 1. Tim. 3. 4. Reg. 19.\+it*\f* warrant. \it Of this ſpouſe all the Prophets writ\it*, & that more plainly than of Chriſt himſelf, \it foreſeeing more adverſaries bending their forces againſt her\it*, as S. Auguſtine obſerveth, \it than againſt Chriſt\it* her head. And the ſame holy father in many places teacheth, that she \it neither perisheth nor loſeth her beauty\it* for the mixture of evil members, in reſpect of whom she \it is blacke, but fayre\it* in reſpect of the good, Cantic. 1. Notwithſtanding therefore ſinners remaining within the Church, ſchiſmatics and heretics breaking from the Church, ſtill she remaineth the \it pillar and firmament of truth, the virgin daughter of Sion\it*.
\c 1
\cd Cyrus king of Perſia moued by diuine inſpiration, releaſeth Gods people from captiuitie, with licenſe to returne and build the Temple in Ieruſalem: 7. reſtoring the holie veſſel, which Nabuchodonoſor had taken from thence.
\p
\v 1 IN \x - \xt Iſa. 44. 45. Jer. 25. 29.\x*\f 2 \fr 1.1 \ft The firſt part. The return of God's people from Babylon.\f* THE firſt yeare of Cyrus king of the Perſians, that the word of our Lord by the mouth of Ieremie might be accomplishd, our Lord rayſed vp the ſpirit of Cyrus king of Perſians: and he made proclamation in al his kingdom, yea by wryting, ſaying:
\v 2 Thus ſayth Cyrus king of the Perſians: Al the kingdomes of the earth hath the Lord the God of heauen geuen me, & he hath commanded me that I should build him a houſe in Ieruſalem, which is in Iewrie.
\v 3 Who is there among you of al his people? His God be with him. Let him goe vp into Ieruſalem, which is in Iewrie, and build the houſe of the Lord the God of Iſrael, he is the God that is in Ieruſalem.
\v 4 And let al the reſt in al places wherſoeuer they dwel, let euery man of his place helpe him, with ſiluer and gold, and ſubſtance, and cattel, beſides that which they offer voluntarily to the temple of God, which is in Ieruſalem.
\v 5 And there roſe vp the princes of the fathers of Iuda and Beniamin, & the Prieſtes, and Leuites, and euerie one, whoſe ſpirit God rayſed vp, to goe vp to build the temple of our Lord, which was in Ieruſalem.
\v 6 And al that were round about, \f a \fr 1.6 \ft Liberally gaue ſuch thinges into their handes.\f* did helpe their handes in veſſels of ſiluer, and of gold, in ſubſtance, and beaſtes, in furniture, beſides thoſe thinges which they had offered voluntarily.
\v 7 King Cyrus alſo brought forth the veſſels of the temple of our Lord, which Nabuchodonoſor had taken of Ieruſalem, and had put them in the temple of his God.
\v 8 But Cyrus the king of Perſians brought them forth by the hand of Mithridates the ſonne of Gazabar, & numbred them to Saſſabaſar the prince of Iuda.
\v 9 And this is the number of them; Phials of gold thirtie, phials of ſiluer a thouſand, kniues twentie nine, goblettes of gold thirtie,
\v 10 goblettes of ſiluer of the ſecond order, foure hundred tenne: other veſſels a thouſand.
\v 11 Al the veſſels of gold and ſiluer, fiue thouſand foure hundred: Saſſabaſar tooke al with them, that went vp from the tranſmigration of Babylon into Ieruſalem.
\c 2
\cd The names and number of ſpecial men, which returned vnder the conduct of Zorobabel into Ieruſalem. 66. their ſubſtance of cattel, 68. and their oblations for the reedifying of the Temple.
\p
\v 1 AND \f a \fr 2.1 \ft This enumeration of the Iſraelites, which aſcended into Ieruſalem, ſignifieth the Elect which aſcend from the militant Chuch to the triumphant.\f* theſe are the children of the prouince, that went vp from the captiuitie, which Nabuchodonoſor the king of Babylon had tranſported into Babylon, and returned into Ieruſalem and Iuda, euerie man into his citie.
\v 2 They that came with Zorobabel, Ioſue, Nehemia, Saraia, Rahelaia, Mardochai, Belſan, Meſphar, Beguai, Rehum, Baana: The number of the men of the people of Iſrael:
\v 3 The children of Pharos two thouſand an hundred ſeuentie two.
\v 4 The children of Sephatia, three hundred ſeuentie two.
\v 5 The children of Area, ſeuen hundred ſeuentie fiue.
\v 6 The children of Phahath Moab, of the children of Ioſue: Iohab, two thouſand eight hundred twelue.
\v 7 The children of Aelam, a thouſand two hundred fiftie foure.
\v 8 The children of Zethua, nine hundred fourtie fiue.
\v 9 The children of Zachai, ſeuen hundred ſixtie.
\v 10 The children of Bani ſix hundred fourtie two.
\v 11 The children of Bebai, ſix hundred twentie three.
\v 12 The children of Azgad, a thouſand two hundred twentie two.
\v 13 The children of Adonicam, ſix hundred ſixtie ſix.
\v 14 The children of Beguai, two thouſand fiftie ſix.
\v 15 The children of Adin, foure hundred fiftie foure.
\v 16 The children of Ather, which were of Ezechias, ninetie eight.
\v 17 The children of Beſai, three hundred and twentie three.
\v 18 The children of Iora, an hundred twelue.
\v 19 The childre of Haſum, two hundred twentie three.
\v 20 The children of Gebbar, ninetie fiue.
\v 21 The children of Bethlehem, an hundred twentie three.
\v 22 The men of Netupha, fiftie ſix.
\v 23 The men of Anathoth an hundred twentie eight.
\v 24 The children of Azmaueth, fourtie two.
\v 25 The children of Cariathiarim, Cephira, and Beroth, ſeuen hundred fourtie three.
\v 26 The children of Rama and Gabaa, ſix hundred twentie one.
\v 27 The men of Machmas, an hundred twentie two.
\v 28 The men of Bethel and Hai, two hundred twentie three.
\v 29 The children of Nebo, fiftie two.
\v 30 The children of Megbis, an hundred fiftie ſix.
\v 31 The children of an other Ælam, a thouſand two hundred fiftie foure.
\v 32 The children of Harim, three hundred twentie.
\v 33 The children of Lod Hadid and Ono, ſeuen hundred twentie fiue.
\v 34 The children of Iericho, three hundred fourtie fiue.
\v 35 The children of Senaa, three thouſand ſix hundred thirtie.
\v 36 The Prieſtes: The children of Iadaia in the houſe of Ioſue, nine hundred ſeuentie three.
\v 37 The children of Emmer, a thouſand fiftie two.
\v 38 The children of Pheshur, a thouſand two hundred fourtie ſeuen.
\v 39 The children of Harim, a thouſand and ſeuentie.
\v 40 The Leuites: The children of Ioſue and Cedmiel, the children of Odouia, ſeuentie foure.
\v 41 The ſinging men: The children of Aſaph, an hundred twentie eight.
\v 42 The children of the Porters: the children of Sellum, the children of Ater, the childreu of Telmon, the children of Accub, the children of Hatita, the children of Sobai: al an hundred thirtie nine.
\v 43 The Natheneites: The children of Siha, the children of Haſupha, the children of Tabbaoth,
\v 44 the children of Ceros, the children of Siaa, the children of Phadon,
\v 45 The children of Lebana, the children of Hagaba, the children of Accub,
\v 46 The children of Hagab, the children of Semlai, the children of Hanan,
\v 47 The children of Gaddel, the children of Gaher, the children of Raaia,
\v 48 The children of Raſin, the children of Necoda, the children of Gazam,
\v 49 The children of Aza, the children of Phaſea, the children of Beſee,
\v 50 The children of Aſena, the children of Munim, the children Nephuſim,
\v 51 The children of Bacbuc, the children of Hacupha, the children of Harhur,
\v 52 The children of Beſluth, the children of Mahida, the children of Harſa,
\v 53 The children of Bercos, the children of Siſara, the children of Thema,
\v 54 The children of Naſia, the children of Hatipha,
\v 55 The children of the ſeruants of Salomon, the children of Sotai, the children of Sophereth, the children of Pharuda,
\v 56 The children of Iala, the children of Dercon, the childrē of Geddel,
\v 57 The children of Saphatia, the children of Hatil, the children of Phochereth, which were of Aſebaim, the children of Ami.
\v 58 Al the Nathineites, and the children of the ſeruantes of Salomon, three hundred ninetie two.
\v 59 And theſe are they that came vp from Thelmela, Thelharſa, Cherub, and Adon, and Emer. And they could not shew the houſe of their fathers and their ſeede, whether they were of Iſrael.
\v 60 The children of Dalaia, the children of Tobia, the children of Necoda, ſix hundred fiftie two.
\v 61 And of the children of the Prieſtes: The children of Hobia, the children of Accos, the children of Berzellai, who tooke a wife of the daughters of Berzellai the Galaadite, and was called by their name:
\v 62 theſe ſought the writing of their genealogie, and \f b \fr 2.62 \ft Such as ſay they are prieſtes and can not ſhew their vocation, muſt not exerciſe. that functon.\f* found it not, and they were caſt out of the Prieſthood.
\v 63 And Atherſatha ſaid to them, that they should not eate of the Holie of holies, til there roſe a prieſt learned and perfect.
\v 64 Al the multitude as it were one man, \f c \fr 2.64 \ft Al aboue numbred of the tribes of Iuda, Beniamin, & Leui, do not amount to 30. thouſand three hundred. So in this general number are contained aboue twelue thouſand of other tribes, not recited among the reſt. as Rabbi Salomoa explicateth the difficultie.\f* fourtie two thouſand three hundred ſixtie:
\v 65 beſide their men ſeruantes, & wemen ſeruantes, which were ſeuen thouſand three hundred thirtie ſeuen: and among them ſinging men, and ſinging wemen two hundred.
\v 66 Their horſes ſeuen hundred thirtie ſix, their mules two hundred fourtie fiue,
\v 67 their cameles, foure hundred thirtie fiue, their aſſes ſix thouſand ſeuen hundred twentie.
\v 68 And of the princes of the fathers, when they entred into the temple of our Lord, which is in Ieruſalem, they offered voluntarily vnto the houſe of our Lord to build it in his place.
\v 69 According to their abilities, they gaue the expenſes of the worke, of gold ſixtie one thouſand ſoldes, of ſiluer fiue thouſand poundes, & garmentes for the prieſtes an hundred.
\v 70 The Prieſtes therfore & the Leuites, and they of the people, and the ſinging men, and the porters, and the Nathinaites dwelt in their cities, and al Iſrael in their cities.
\c 3
\cd An Altar is built for ſacrifice, 4 The feaſt of Tabernacles ſolemnly celebrated 8. And in the ſecond yeare ( after their returne ) the Temple is founded with great ioy of the people, and mourning of ſome.
\p
\v 1 AND now the ſeuenth moneth was come, and the children of Iſrael were in their cities: the people therfore was gathered together as it were one man into Ieruſalem.
\v 2 And Ioſue the ſonne of Ioſedec roſe vp, and his brethren the Prieſtes, and Zorobabel the ſonne of Salathiel, and his brethren, and they built the altar of the God of Iſrael, that they might offer on it holocauſtes, as it is writen in the law of Moyſes the man of God.
\v 3 And they placed the altar of God vpon his feete, the people of the lands round about putting them \f a \fr 3.3 \ft Notwithſtanding the terrour of infideles, Gods ſeruantes tooke corage to offer ſacrifice.\f* in feare, & they offered vpon it holocauſt to our Lord morning and euening.
\v 4 And they made the ſolemnitie of tabernacles, as it is writen, and holocauſt euery day by order according to the præcept, the worke of the day in his day.
\v 5 And after theſe thinges the continual holocauſt, as wel in the Calendes, as in al the ſolemnities of our Lord, that were conſecrated, and in al wherein there was offered voluntarily a gift to our Lord.
\v 6 From the firſt day of the ſeuenth moneth they began to offer holocauſt to our Lord: moreouer the temple of God was not yet founded.
\v 7 And they gaue money to hewers of ſtones and to maſons: meate alſo and drinke, and oyle to the Sidonians and Tyrians, that they should bring ceder trees from Libanus to the ſea vnto Ioppe, according to that which Cyrus the king of the Perſians had commanded them.
\v 8 And in the ſecond yeare of their coming to the temple of God in Ieruſalem, the ſecond moneth, began Zorobabel the ſonne of Salathiel, and Ioſue the ſonne of Ioſedec, and the reſt of their brethren the Prieſtes, and the Leuites, and al that were come from the captiuitie into Ieruſalem, and they appoynted Leuites from twentie yeares and vpward, that they ſhould haſten forward the worke of our Lord.
\v 9 And Ioſue ſtood and his ſonnes, and his brethren, Cedmihel, and his ſonnes, and the children of Iuda, as it were one man, that they might be inſtant vpon them, that did the worke in the temple of God: the ſonnes of Henadad, and their ſonnes, and their brethren Leuites.
\v 10 The temple therfore of our Lord being founded by the maſons, the Prieſtes ſtood in their attyre with trumpettes: and the Leuites the children of Aſaph in cymbals, to prayſe God \f b \fr 3.10 \ft By the ordinance of Dauid. \+it 1. Par. 25\+it*\f* by the handes of Dauid the king of Iſrael.
\v 11 And they ſang together in hymnes, and confeſſion to our Lord: Becauſe he is good, becauſe his mercie is for euer vpon Iſrael. Al the people alſo made a ſhoute with a lowde crie in prayſing our Lord, becauſe the temple of our Lord was founded.
\v 12 Verie manie alſo of the Prieſtes and the Leuites, and the princes of the fathers, & the ancients, that had ſeene the former temple; when they ſaw this temple founded, \f c \fr 3.12 \ft By their weping they teſtified that the new temple was not ſo excellent as the former. And therfore Aggeus prophecie ( \+it c. 2\+it*. ) can not be vnderſtood of this temple, but of the Church of Chriſt. \+it S Aug. li. 18. ciuit. c. 45\+it*.\f* they wept with a lowd voyce: and manie ſhouting in ioy, lifted vp their voyce.
\v 13 Neither could anie man diſcerne the voice of the crie of them that reioyced, and the voice of the weeping of the people: for one with an other the people showted with a lowd crie, and the voice was heard far of.
\c 4
\cd The ſchiſmatical Samaritanes, becauſe they are not admitted to communicate with the Iewes, endeuour to hinder the building of the Temple. 5. which neuertheles proceedeth, al the dayes of king Cyrus. 7. but is hundered by Artaxerxes, til the ſecond yeare of Darius.
\p
\v 1 BVT the enemies of Iuda and Beniamin heard that the children of the captiuitie built a temple to our Lord the God of Iſrael.
\v 2 And coming to Zorobabel, and the princes of the fathers, they ſayd to them: Let vs build with you, becauſe \f a \fr 4.2 \ft Schiſmatikes and Heretikes may not be admitted to communicate in ſacrifice with Catholiques.\f* euen as you, ſo do we ſeeke your God: Behold we haue immolated victimes from the dayes of Aſor Haddan the king of Aſſur, which brought vs hither.
\v 3 And Zorobabel ſayd to them, and Ioſue, and the reſt of the princes of the fathers of Iſrael: It is not for you and vs to build a houſe to our God, but we our ſelues alone wil build to the Lord our God, as Cyrus the king of the Perſians hath cōmanded vs.
\v 4 It came to paſſe therfore that the people of the land hindred the handes of the people of Iuda, and trubled them in building.
\v 5 And they hyred counſelers agaynſt them, to deſtroy theyr counſel al the dayes of Cyrus the king of the Perſians, & vntil the reigne of Darius the king of the Perſians.
\v 6 And in the reigne of Aſſuerus in the begining of his reigne, they wrote an accuſation againſt the inhabitantes of Iuda and Ieruſalem.
\v 7 And in the dayes of Artaxerxes Beſelam, Mitridates, & Thabeel, & the reſt that were in theyr counſel writ to Artaxerxes king of the Perſians: and the epiſtle of the accuſation was writen in Syriake, and was read in the Sirian language.
\v 8 Reum Beelteem, and Samſai ſcribe wrote one epiſtle from Ieruſalem to Artaxerxes the king, of this tenure:
\v 9 Reum Beelteem, and Samſai ſcribe, and the reſt of their counſelers, the Dineites, and the Apharſathaceites, the Terphaleites, the the Apharſeites, the Erchueites; the Babylonians, the Suſanecheites, the Dieuites, and the Aelamites,
\v 10 and the reſt of the Gentiles, which Aſenaphar the great and glorious tranſported: and made them dwel in the cities of Samaria, and in the reſt of the countries beyond the Riuer in peace:
\v 11 ( this is the copie of the epiſtle, which they ſent to him) To Artaxerxes the king, thy ſeruantes, the men that are beyond the Riuer, ſend greeting.
\v 12 Be it knowen to the king, that the Iewes, which came vp from thee to vs, are come into Ieruſalem a rebellious and naughtie citie, which they build making the rampyres thereof, and repayring the walles.
\v 13 Now therfore be it knowen to the king, that if that citie ſhal be built, and the walles therof repayred, they will not geue tribute, and tolle, & yearly rentes, and this damage wil come euen to the kinges.
\v 14 But we mindful of the ſalt that we haue eaten in the palace, and becauſe we count it heynous to ſee the kings harmes, therfore we haue ſent and certified the king,
\v 15 that thou recount in the bookes of the hiſtories of thy fathers, and thou shalt finde writen in the commentaries: and shalt know that that citie is a rebellious citie, and hurtful to the kinges, and prouinces, and battels are rayſed in it of old time: for the which cauſe alſo the citie it ſelfe was deſtroyed.
\v 16 We certifie the king, that if that citie shal be built, and the walles therof repayred, thou shalt haue no poſſeſſion beyond the Riuer.
\v 17 The king ſent word to Reum Beelteem, and Samſai ſcribe, and to the reſt that were in their counſel inhabitantes of Samaria, and to the reſt beyond the Riuer, ſending greeting and peace.
\v 18 The accuſation, which you haue ſent to vs, was openly read before me.
\v 19 and I gaue commandment: and they recounted, and haue found, that that citie of old time rebelleth agaynſt the kinges, and ſeditions and battels are rayſed in it.
\v 20 For there haue beene alſo moſt valiant kinges in Ieruſalem, which alſo had dominion ouer al the countrie, that is beyond the Riuer. They tooke alſo tribute, and tolle, and rentes.
\v 21 Now therfore heare the ſentence: Prohibite ye thoſe men, that that citie be not built, til it perhaps ſhal be commanded by me.
\v 22 See that you doe not negligently accomplish this thing; and by litle there grow euil agaynſt the kinges.
\v 23 Therfore the copie of the edict of Artaxerxes the king was read before Reum Beelteem, and Samſai the ſcribe, and their counſelers: and they went in haſt into Ieruſalem to the Iewes, & prohibited them with arme and ſtrength.
\v 24 Then was the worke of the houſe of our Lord in Ieruſalem intermitted, and was not done vntil the ſecond yeare of the reigne of Darius the king of the Perſians.
\c 5
\cd By the exhortation of Aggæus, and Zacharias, the people procede in building the Temple. 3. VVhich their enimies ſtriuing to hinder, 5. for deciſion of the cauſe, both parties write to king Darius.
\p
\v 1 AND there prophecied Aggeus the Prophete, & Zacharias the ſonne of Addo, prophecying to the Iewes, that were in Iewrie and Ieruſalem, in the name of the God of Iſrael.
\v 2 Then roſe vp Zorobabel the ſonne of Salathiel, and Ioſue the ſonne of Ioſedec, and began to build the temple of God in Ieruſalem, and with them the prophetes of God helping them.
\v 3 But at the ſame time there came to them Thathanai, who was prince beyond the Riuer, and Stharbuzanai, and their counſelers: and ſayd thus to them: Who hath geuen you counſel to build this houſe, and to repayre the walles?
\v 4 Wherto we anſwered them, what the names were of the men that were authors of that building.
\v 5 And \f a \fr 5.5 \ft God geuing corege to his ſeruantes, ſtricke their enemies with terrour, and ſo made them ceaſe from hinderuig his worke as they before intended.\f* the eye of theyr God was ſet vpon the ancientes of the Iewes, and they could not inhibite them. And it pleaſed them that the matter ſhould be referred to Darius, and then they would ſatisfie agaynſt that accuſation.
\v 6 The copie of the epiſtle, which Thathanai prince of the countrie beyond the Riuer ſent, and Stharbuzanai, and his counſelers the Arphaſacheites, which were beyond the Riuer, to Darius the king.
\v 7 The word, which they ſent him, was writen thus: To Darius the king al peace.
\v 8 Be it knowen to the king, that we went to Iurie, the prouince, to the houſe of the great God, which is in building with ſtone vnpolished, and timber is put in the walles: and that worke is in building diligently, and groweth in their handes.
\v 9 We therfore demanded of thoſe ancientes; and thus we ſayd to them: Who hath geuen you authoritie to build this houſe, & to repaire theſe walles?
\v 10 Yea and their names we asked of them, that we might certifie thee: and we writte the names of thoſe men, that are the chiefe among them.
\v 11 And they anſwered vs theſe wordes, ſayng: We are the ſeruantes of the God of heauen & earth, and we do build a temple, that was built theſe manie yeares before, and which a great king of Iſrael built and ſet vp.
\v 12 But after that our fathers prouoked the God of heauen to wrath, he deliuered them into the handes of Nabuchodonoſor the king of Babylon the Chaldee: this houſe alſo he deſtroyed, and his people he tranſported into Babylon.
\v 13 But in the firſt yeare of Cyrus the king of Babylon, Cyrus the king put forth an edict, that this houſe of God should be built.
\v 14 For the veſſeles alſo of the temple of God, of gold and of ſiluer, which Nabuchodonoſor had taken out of the temple, that was in Ieruſalem, and had caried them into the temple of Babylon, Cyrus the king brought forth out of the temple of Babylon, and they were geuen to Saſſabaſar ſo called, whom alſo he appointed the chiefe,
\v 15 and ſayd to him: Take theſe veſſels, and goe, and put them in the temple, that is in Ieruſalem, and let the houſe of God be built in his place.
\v 16 Then therfore came this Saſſabaſar, and layd the foundations of the temple of God in Ieruſalem, and from that time vntil now it is in building, and is not yet finished.
\v 17 Now therfore if it ſeeme good to the king, let him ſearch in the kings librarie, which is in Babylon, whether it hath beene commanded by Cyrus the king, that the houſe of God in Ieruſalem should be built, and let him ſend the kings pleaſure concerning this thing vnto vs.
\c 6
\cd Darius finding in the regiſter, that Cyrus gaue licence to build the Temple, commandeth that none hinder it. 8. geueth alſo money towardes the charges, and hoſtes for ſacrifice.
\p
\v 1 THen Darius the king commanded, and they ſearched in the librarie of the bookes, that were layd vp in Babylon,
\v 2 and there was found in Ecbatanis, which is a caſtle in the prouince Medena, one volume, and there was ſuch a cōmentarie writen therein:
\v 3 In the firſt yeare of Cyrus the king: Cyrus the king decreed, that the houſe of God should be built, which is in Ieruſalem, in the place where they immolate hoſtes, and that they lay the foundations ſupporting the height of threeſcore cubites, & the bredth of threeſcore cubites,
\v 4 three rewes of ſtones vnpolished, & ſo rewes of new timber: and the coſtes shal be geuen out of the kings houſe.
\v 5 Yea & the veſſels of the temple of God, of gold & of ſiluer which Nabuchodonoſor had taken out of the Temple of Ieruſalem, and had brought them into Babylon, let them be reſtored,& brought backe into the temple of Ieruſalem vnto their place, which alſo were put in the temple of God.
\v 6 Now therfore Thathanai prince of the countrie, that is beyond the Riuer, Stharubazanai, and your counſelers the Apharſacheites, which are beyond the Riuer, depart farre from them,
\v 7 and ſuffer that temple of God to be made of the duke of the Iewes, and of their ancientes, that they may build that houſe of God in his place.
\v 8 Alſo there is cōmandment geuen from me what muſt be done of thoſe ancientes of the Iewes, that the houſe of God may be built, to witte, that of the kings coffer, that is, of the tributes, that are geuen out of the countrie beyond the Riuer, the charges be diligently geuen to thoſe men, leſt the worke be hindred.
\v 9 And if it ſhal be neceſſarie, calues alſo, and lambes, and kiddes, for holocauſt to the God of heauen, wheate, ſalt, wyne, and oyle, according to the rite of the Prieſtes, that are in Ieruſalem, let there be geuen them day dy day, that there be no complaynte in any thing.
\v 10 And let them offer oblations to the God of heauen, and pray for the life of the king, and of his children.
\v 11 By me therfore there is a decree made; That euerie man, which ſhal alter this commandement, there be a beame taken of his houſe, and ſet vp, and he be faſt hanged vpon it, and his houſe be confiſcate.
\v 12 And the God, that hath made his name to dwel there, deſtroy al kingdomes, and the people that shal extend theyr hand to reſiſt, & to deſtroy the houſe of God, that is in Ieruſalé. I Darius haue made the decree, which I wil to be diligently accōplished.
\v 13 Therfore Thathanai the prince of the countrie beyond the Riuer, and Stharbuzani, and his counſelers, according to that which Darius the king had commanded, ſo did execute it diligently.
\v 14 And the ancientes of the Iewes built, and proſpered according to the prophecie of Aggeus the prophet, and of Zacharias the ſonne of Addo: and they built and ſet vp, the God of Iſrael commanding, and \f a \fr 6.14 \ft The hart of the king is in the hand of our Lord. \+it Prouerb. 21\+it*.\f* Cyrus commanding, and Darius, and Artaxerxes the kings of the Perſians.
\v 15 And they were finiſhing this houſe of God, vntil the third day of the moneth of Adar, which is the ſixth yeare of Darius the king.
\v 16 And the children of Iſrael, the Prieſtes and the Leuites, and the reſt of the tranſmigration, made the dedication of the houſe of God in ioy.
\v 17 And they offered in the dedication of the houſe of God, calues an hundred, rammes two hundred, lambes foure hundred, buckgoates for the ſinne of al Iſrael twelue, according to the number of the tribes of Iſrael.
\v 18 And they ſet the Prieſtes in theyr orders, and the Leuites in theyr courſes ouer the workes of God in Ieruſalem, as it is writen in the booke of Moyſes.
\v 19 And the children of Iſrael of the tranſmigration made the Phaſe, the fourtenth day of the firſt moneth,
\v 20 For al the Prieſtes and the Leuites were purified as it were one man: al cleane to immolate the Phaſe for al the children of the tranſmigration, and for theyr brethren the Prieſtes, and them ſelues.
\v 21 And the children of Iſrael that were returned from the tranſmigration, did eate, and al that had ſeparated them ſelues from the coinquination of the Gentiles of the earth vnto them, to ſeeke our Lord the God of Iſrael.
\v 22 And they made the ſolemnitie of Azymes ſeuen dayes in ioy, becauſe our Lord had made them ioyful, and had turned the hart of the king of Aſſur to them, that he ſhould helpe theyr handes in the worke of the houſe of our Lord the God of Iſrael.
\c 7
\cd Eſdras, with manie other Prieſtes and Leuites, aſcendeth to Ieruſalem to teach, and aſſiſt the people. 11. bringing Artaxerxes Edict, declareth it to the people, 27. and geueth thankes to God.
\p
\v 1 AND \f 1 \fr 7.1 \ft The ſecond part. Eſdras inſtructeth the people.\f* after theſe thinges in the reigne of Artaxerxes king of Perſians, Eſdras the ſonne of Saraias, the ſonne of Azarias, the ſonne of Helcias,
\v 2 the ſonne of Sellum, the ſonne of Sadoc, the ſonne of Achitob,
\v 3 the ſonne of Amarias, the ſonne of Azarias, the ſonne of Maraioth,
\v 4 the ſonne of Zarahias, the ſonne of Ozi, the ſonne of Bocci,
\v 5 the ſonne of Abiſue, the ſonne of Phinees, the ſonne of Eleazar, the ſonne of Aaron the Prieſt from the begynning.
\v 6 The ſame Eſdras \f a \fr 7.6 \ft Eſdras came with the firſt from Babylon \+it (2.Eſd.12\+it*.) but returned thither and now aſcended the ſecond time to Ieruſalem.\f* came vp from Babylon, and he was a quicke ſcribe in the law of Moyſes, which our Lord God gaue to Iſrael: and the king gaue him according to the hand of our Lord his God vpon him al his petition.
\v 7 And there came vp of the children of Iſrael, and of the children of the Prieſtes, and of the children of the Leuites, and of the ſinging men, and of the porters, and of the Nathineites into Ieruſalem in the ſeuenth yeare of Artaxerxes the king.
\v 8 And they came into Ieruſalem the fifth moneth, that is the ſeuenth yeare of the king.
\v 9 For in the firſt day of the firſt moneth he began to goe vp from Babylon, and in the firſt day of the fifth moneth he came into Ieruſalem according to the good hand of his God vpon him.
\v 10 For Eſdras prepared his hart to ſearch the law of our Lord, and to doe and to teach in Iſrael preceptes and iudgement.
\v 11 And this is the copie of the epiſtle of the edict, which king Artaxerxes gaue to Eſdras the Prieſt, the learned ſcribe, in the wordes and preceptes of our Lord, and his ceremonies in Iſrael.
\v 12 Artaxerxes the king of kings to Eſdras the Prieſt, the moſt learned ſcribe of the law of God of heauē, greeting.
\v 13 It is decreed by me that whōſoeuer it shal pleaſe in my kingdom of the people of Iſrael, and of the Prieſtes and Leuites, to goe into Ieruſalē, let him goe with thee.
\v 14 For thou art ſent from the face of the king, and of his ſeuen counſelers, that thou mayſt viſite Iewrie and Ieruſalem in the law of thy God, which is in thy hand.
\v 15 And that thou maiſt carie the ſiluer & gold, which the king & his counſelers haue voluntarily offered to the God of Iſrael, whoſe tabernacle is in Ieruſalem.
\v 16 And al the ſiluer and gold whatſoeuer thou ſhalt finde in al the prouince of Babylon, and the people wil offer, and of the Prieſtes that shal voluntarely offer to the houſe of theyr God, which is in Ieruſalem,
\v 17 take freely, and bye diligently of this money calues, rammes, lambes, and the ſacrifices and libamentes of them, and offer them vpon the altar of the temple of your God, that is in Ieruſalem.
\v 18 Yea and if it shal pleaſe thee, and thy brethren to doe any thing with the reſt of the ſiluer and gold, doe ye according to the wil of your God.
\v 19 The veſſels alſo, which are geuen thee for the miniſterie of the houſe of thy God, deliuer thou in the ſight of God in Ieruſalem.
\v 20 Yea and other thinges wherof neede shal be for the houſe of thy God, how much ſoeuer is neceſſarie for thee to ſpend, thou shalt geue it out of the treaſure, and excheker of the king, and from me.
\v 21 I Artaxerxes the king haue appointed and decreed to al the keepers of the common coffer, that are beyond the Riuer, that whatſoeuer Eſdras the Prieſt, the ſcribe of the law of God of heauen, shal aske of you, you geue it without delay,
\v 22 vnto an hundred talentes of ſiluer, and vnto an hundred cores of wheat, and vnto an hundred bates of wyne, and vnto an hundred bates of oyle, but ſalt without meaſure.
\v 23 Al that pertayneth to the rite of the God of heauen, let it be geuen diligently in the houſe of the God of heauen: leſt perhaps he be angrie agaynſt the kingdom of the king, and of his ſonnes.
\v 24 We doe you alſo to vnderſtand concerning al the Prieſtes, and Leuites, and the ſingers, and the porters, the Nathineites, and miniſters of the houſe of this God, that you haue no authoritie to put tolle and tribute, and yearlie rentes vpon them.
\v 25 And thou Eſdras according to the wiſedom of thy God, which is in thy hand, appoynt iudges and preſidentes, that they may iudge for al the people, that is beyond the Riuer, that is for them which know the law of thy God, yea and the ignorant teach ye frely.
\v 26 And euerie one that shal not doe the law of thy God, and the law of thy king diligently, there shal be iudgement of him, either vnto death, or into banishment, or to the confiſcation of his ſubſtance, or at the leaſt into priſon.
\v 27 Bleſſed be our Lord, the God of our fathers, which hath put this in the kinges hart, that he would glorifie the houſe of our Lord, which is in Ieruſalem,
\v 28 and hath inclined his mercie toward me before the king and his counſelers, and al the mightie princes of the king: and I taking courage by the hand of our Lord my God, which was on me, gathered together out of Iſrael princes that should goe vp with me.
\c 8
\cd Eſdras reciteth thoſe that came with him from Babylon. 21. the faſt which he appointed. 33. and how they brought the holie veſſel into the Temple.
\p
\v 1 THESE therfore are the princes of the families, and the genealogie of them, that came vp with me in the reigne of Artaxerxes the king \f a \fr 8.1 \ft This great number which by Eſdras perſwaſion came from Babylon, ſignified the great fruict of ſoules conuerted from ſinne by the exhortation of holie preachers. \+it S. Beda. li. 2. in Eſdra. c. 10\+it*.\f* out of Babylon.
\v 2 Of the children of Phinees, Gerſom. Of the children of Ithamar, Daniel. Of the children of Dauid, Hattus.
\v 3 Of the children of Sechenias, the children of Pharos, Zacharias: and with him were numbered an hundred fiftie men.
\v 4 Of the children of Phahath Moab, Eleoenai the ſonne of Zareha, and with him two hundred men.
\v 5 Of the children of Sechenias, the ſonne of Ezechiel, and with him three hundred men.
\v 6 Of the children of Adan, Abed the ſonne of Ionathan, and with him fiftie men.
\v 7 Of the children of Alam, Iſaias the ſonne of Athalia, and with him ſeuentie men.
\v 8 Of the children of Saphatia, Zebedia the ſonne of Michael, and with him eightie men.
\v 9 Of the children of Ioab, Obedia the ſonne of Iahiel, and with him two hundred and eightene men.
\v 10 Of the children of Selomith, the ſonne of Ioſphia, and with him an hundred ſixtie men.
\v 11 Of the children of Bebai, Zacharias the ſonne of Bebai: and with him twentie eight men.
\v 12 Of the children of Azgad, Ioanan the ſonne of Eccetan, and with him an hundred and ten men.
\v 13 Of the children of Adonicam, which were the laſt: and theſe are theyr names: Elipheleth, and Iehiel, and Samaias, and with them ſixtie men.
\v 14 Of the children of Begui, Vthai and Zachur, and with them ſeuentie men.
\v 15 And I gathered them to the riuer, which runneth downe to Ahaua, and we taried there three daies: and I ſought among the people and among the Prieſtes for the children of Leui, and found none there.
\v 16 Therfore I ſent Eliezer, and Ariel, and Semeias, and Elnathan, and Iarib, and other Elnathan, and Nathan, and Zacharias, and Moſollam princes: and Ioiarib, and Elnathan wiſe men.
\v 17 And I ſent them to Eddo, which is chiefe in the place of Chaſphia, and I did put in theyr mouth the wordes that they should ſpeake to Eddo, and his brethren the Nathineites in the place of Chaſphia, that they should bring vs miniſters of the houſe of our God.
\v 18 And they brought vs by the good hand of our God vpon vs a moſt learned man of the children of Mocholi the ſonne of Leui the ſonne of Iſrael, and Sarabias and his ſonnes, aud his brethren eightene,
\v 19 and Haſabias, and with him Iſaias of the children of Merari, and his brethren, and his ſonnes twentie.
\v 20 And al the Nathineites, which Dauid gaue, and the princes for the miniſteries of the Leuites, Natineites two hundred twentie: al theſe were called by theyr names.
\v 21 And I proclaymed there \f b \fr 8.21 \ft It ſufficeth not to part from Babylon (that is, from ſinne) but we muſt alſo doe workes of ſatisfaction, and therfore Eſdras here proclamed an extraordinarie faſt to thoſe that were come from captiuitie.\f* a faſt beſide the Riuer of Ahaua, that we might be afflicted before the Lord our God, and might deſire of him a right way for vs and our children, and al our ſubſtance.
\v 22 For I was aſhamed to aſke the king ayde and horſemen, that might defend vs from the enemie in the way: becauſe we had ſayd to the king: The hand of our God is vpon al them, that ſeeke him in goodneſſe: and his empire and ſtrength, and furie vpon al them that forſake him.
\v 23 And we faſted, and beſought our God hereby: and it fel out proſperouſely vnto vs.
\v 24 And I ſeparated twelue of the chief Prieſtes, Sarabias, and Haſabias, and with them ten of theyr brethren.
\v 25 And I weyed vnto them the ſiluer and gold, and the conſecrated veſſels of the houſe of our God, which the king had offered and his counſelers, and his princes, and al Iſrael of them, that were found:
\v 26 and I weyed in theyr handes of ſiluer ſix hundred fiftie talentes, and veſſels of ſiluer an hundred, of gold an hundred talentes:
\v 27 and cuppes of gold twentie, which had a thouſand ſolides, and two veſſels of the beſt ſhyning braſſe, fayre, as gold.
\v 28 And I ſayd to them: You are the holie of our Lord, and the veſſels are holie, and the ſiluer and gold, that is voluntarely offered to our Lord the God of our fathers:
\v 29 Watch & keepe it, vntil you deliuer it by weight before the princes of the Prieſtes, and of the Leuites, and the princes of the families of Iſrael in Ieruſalem, into the treaſure of the houſe of our Lord.
\v 30 And the Prieſtes and the Leuites receiued the weight of the ſiluer and gold, and of the veſſels, to carie it to Ieruſalem into the houſe of our God.
\v 31 We therfore did ſette forward from the riuer of Ahaua the twelfth day of the firſt moneth to goe on to Ieruſalem: and the hand of our God was vpon vs, and deliuered vs from the hand of the enemie, and the lyer in wayte by the way.
\v 32 And we came to Ieruſalem, and we taried there three dayes.
\v 33 And in the fourth day the ſiluer was weyed, and the gold, and the veſſels in the houſe of our God by the hand of Meremoth the ſonne of Vrias the Prieſt, and with him Eleazar the ſonne of Phimees, and with them Iozabed the ſonne of Ioſue, and Noadaia the ſonne of Bemai Leuites,
\v 34 according to the number and weight of al: and al the weight was deſcribed at that tyme.
\v 35 Yea and the children of the tranſmigration that were come from the captiuitie, offered holocauſtes to the God of Iſrael, calues twelue for al the people of Iſrael, rammes nyntie ſix, lambes ſeuentie ſeuen, buckegoates for ſinne twelue: al for holocauſt to our Lord.
\v 36 And they gaue the kings edictes to the princes, that were ouerſeers for the king, and the dukes beyond the Riuer, and they aduanced the people and the houſe of God.
\c 9
\cd For mariages and other aſſociation which the Iewes had with Gentiles, Eſdras lamenteth, 5. and confeſſing the peoples iniquitie, prayeth God to conſerue them from vtter deſtruction.
\p
\v 1 AND after theſe thinges were accomplished, the princes came to me, ſaying: The people of Iſrael, and the Prieſts and Leuites \f a \fr 9.1 \ft Malachias the Prophet complaineth alſo of this fault \+it c. 2, v. 11\+it*. threatning Gods punishment both to ſuperiors and ſubiectes for not correcting it. \+it v, 12\+it*.\f* are not ſeperated from the people of the landes, and from the abhominations of them, to witte, of the Chananeite, and Hetheite, and Pherezeite, and Iebuſeite, and of the Ammonites, and Moabites, and the Ægyptians, and Amorrheites.
\v 2 For they haue taken of their daughters to them and to their ſonnes, and haue mingled the holie ſeede with the people of the landes. The hand alſo of the princes and magiſtrates hath bene firſt in this tranſgreſſion.
\v 3 And when I had heard this word, I rent my cloke and my coate, and plucked of the heares of my head and bearde, and ſate mourning.
\v 4 And there aſſembled to me al that feared the God of Iſrael, for the trangreſſion of them, that were come from the captiuitie, and I ſate ſorowful, vntil the euening ſacrifice.
\v 5 And at the euening ſacrifice I roſe out of my affliction, and my cloke and coate being rent I fell vpon my knees, and ſpred forth my hands to our Lord my God,
\v 6 And ſaid: My God I am confounded and ashamed to lifte vp my face to thee: becauſe our iniquities are multiplied ouer our head, and our ſinnes haue growen euen vnto heauen,
\v 7 from the dayes of our fathers: yea and we our ſelues alſo haue ſinned greuouſly vnto this day, and in our iniquities haue we bene deliuered our ſelues, and our Kinges, and our Prieſtes into the hand of the kinges of the landes, and into the ſword, and into captuitie, and into ſpoyle, and into confuſion of countenance, as alſo at this day.
\v 8 And now as it were a litle, and for a moment was our prayer made before the Lord our God, that a remnant might be left vs, and \f a \fr 9.8 \ft In reſpect of their great iniquities Eſdras preſumeth not to aske the conſeruation of the whole people, but ſome reliques or ſmaleſt part, as it were a little poſt, or a naile of a whole houſe towards the reedifying therof.\f* a nayle might be geuen vs in his holie place, and that our God would illuminate our eies, and would geue vs a litle life in our bondage.
\v 9 Becauſe we are bondmen, and in our bondage our God did not forſake vs, & he inclined mercie vpon vs before the king of the Perſians, to geue vs life, and to aduance the houſe of our God, and to build the deſolations therof and to geue vs a hedge in Iuda and Ieruſalem.
\v 10 And now what shal we ſay, ô our God, after theſe thinges? becauſe we haue forſaken thy cōmandmēts,
\v 11 which thou haſt cōmanded in the hand of thy ſeruantes the prophetes, ſaying: The land, to the which you enter to poſſeſſe it, is an vncleane land, according to the vncleanneſſe of peoples, and of other landes, by the abhominations of them that haue filled it from mouth vnto mouth in theier coinquination.
\v 12 Now therfore geue not your daughters to their ſonnes, and their daughters take not for your ſonnes, and doe ye not ſeeke their peace, and their proſperity for euer: that you may be ſtrengthned, and may eate the goodes that are of the land, and may haue your children heyres for euer.
\v 13 And after al thinges that come vpon vs in our moſt wicked workes, and our moſt great ſinne, becauſe thou our God haſt deliuered vs from our iniquitie, and haſt geuen vs health as it is at this day,
\v 14 that we shal not turne away, and make fruſtrate thy commandementes, neither ſhould ioyne matrimonies with the peoples of theſe abominations. Why art thou angrie with vs vnto vtter deſtruction, not to leaue vs a remnant vnto ſaluation?
\v 15 Lord God of Iſrael thou art iuſt: becauſe we are leift, which ſhould be ſaued as at this day. Behold we are before thee in our ſinne, for there can be no ſtanding before thee vpon this.
\c 10
\cd Eſdras calling the people together commandeth them to diſmiſſe the ſtrange wemen, which they haue married: 14 appointing officers to ſee it executed: 18 and reciteth thoſe which had married ſuch wemen.
\p
\v 1 ESDRAS therfore thus praying, and beſeeching, and weeping, and lying before the temple of God, there was gathered to him of Iſrael an exceeding great companie of men and wemen and children, and the people wept with much lamentation.
\v 2 And Sechenias the ſonne of Iehiel of the children of Aelam anſwered, and ſaid to Eſdras: We haue tranſgreſſed againſt our God, and haue taken to wiues ſtrange wemen of the peoples of the land: and now if there be penance in Iſrael vpon this,
\v 3 let vs make a couenant with the Lord our God, to put away al the wiues, and them that are borne of them, according to the wil of our Lord, and of them that feare the precept of the Lord our God: be it done according to the law.
\v 4 Ariſe, it is thy part to diſcerne, and we wil be with thee: take courage, and doe it.
\v 5 Eſdras therefore roſe vp, and adiured the Princes of the Prieſtes and of the Leuites, and al Iſrael, that they should doe according to this word, and they ſware.
\v 6 And Eſdras roſe vp before the houſe of God, and went to the chamber of \f a \fr 10.6 \ft Eſdras being extraordinarily ſent by God, to correct the people, repayreth to the high Prieſts ſonne, by his authoritie calleth the people together, and ſo procedeth to make reformation. So S. Paul conferred with other Apoſtles. \+it Gal. 2\+it*\f* Iohanan the ſonne of Eliaſib, and entered into it, he did eate no bread, and dranke no water: for he mourned for the tranſgreſſion of them, that were come out of the captiuitie.
\v 7 And there was a proclamation ſent in Iuda and Ieruſalem to al the children of the tranſmigration, that they should aſſemble together into Ieruſalem.
\v 8 And euerie one that shal not come within three dayes, according to the counſel of the princes and ancientes, al his ſubſtance shal be taken away, and him ſelfe shal be caſt out of the companie of the tranſmigration.
\v 9 There aſſembled therfore al the men of Iuda, and Beniamin into Ieruſalem within three dayes, that is the ninth moneth, the twenteth day of the moneth: and al the people ſate in the ſtreate of the houſe of God, trembling for the ſinne, and \f b \fr 10.9 \ft Their ſinne was puniſhed by ouer much rayne. \+it v. 13\+it*. And ſo affliction gaue them more feeling of their faultes.\f* the rayne.
\v 10 And Eſdras the Prieſt aroſe, and ſayd to them: You haue tranſgreſſed, and taken ſtrange wemen to wife, to adde vpon the ſinne of Iſrael.
\v 11 And now geue confeſſion to our Lord the God of your fathers, and doe his pleaſure, and be ſeparated from the peoples of the land, and from your wiues the ſtrangers.
\v 12 And al the multitude anſwered, and ſayd with a lowde voyce: According to thy word vnto vs, ſo be it done.
\v 13 Neuertheleſſe becauſe there is much people, and a tyme of rayne, and we can not abyde to ſtand without, and it is not a worke of one day or two ( for we haue excedingly ſinned in this thing )
\v 14 let there be princes appoynted in al the multitude: and let al in our cities that haue taken ſtrangers to wife, come at ſette tymes, and with them the ancientes by citie and citie, and the iudges therof, vntil the wrath of our God be turned away from vs for this ſinne.
\v 15 Therfore Ionathan the ſonne of Azahel, and Iaazia the ſonne of Thecua were appoynted ouer this, and Meſollam and Sebethai Leuites did helpe them:
\v 16 and the children of the tranſmigration did ſo. And Eſdras the Prieſt, and the men, princes of the families went into the houſes of theyr fathers, and al by theyr names, and they ſate in the firſt day of the tenth moneth to ſearch out the matter.
\v 17 And al the men were fully counted that had taken ſtrangers to wife, vnto the firſt day of the firſt moſneth.
\v 18 And there were found of the ſonnes of the Prieſtes that had taken ſtrangers to wife. Of the children of Ioſue the ſonne of Ioſedec, and his brethren, Maaſia, and Eliezer, and Iarib, and Godolia.
\v 19 And they gaue theyr handes to put away theyr wiues, and to offer for theyr offence a ramme of the flocke.
\v 20 And of the children of Emmer, Hanani, and Zebedia.
\v 21 And of the children of Harim, Maaſia, and Elia, and Semeia, and Iehiel, and Ozias.
\v 22 And of the children of Pheſhur, Elionai, Maaſia, Iſmael, Nathanael, Iozabed, and Elaſa.
\v 23 And of the children of the Leuites, Iozabed, and Semei, and Celaia, the ſame is Calita, Phataia, Iuda, and Eliezer.
\v 24 And of the ſinging men Eliaſib: and of the porters, Sellum, and Thelem, and Vri.
\v 25 And of Iſrael, of the children of Pharos, Remeia, and Iezia, and Melchia, and Miamin, and Eliezer, and Melchia, and Banea.
\v 26 And of the children of Aelam, Mathania, Zacharias, and Iehiel, and Abdi, and Ierimoth, and Elia.
\v 27 And of the children of Zethua, Elioenai, Eliaſib, Mathania, Ierimuth, and Zabad, and Aziza.
\v 28 And of the children of Bebai, Iohanan, Hanamia, Zabbai, Athalai:
\v 29 And of the children of Bani, Moſollam, and Melluch, and Adaia, Iaſub, and Saal, and Ramoth.
\v 30 And of the children of Phahath Moab, Edna and Chalal, Bananias, and Maaſias, Mathanias, Beſeleel, Bennui, and Manaſſe.
\v 31 And of the children of Herem, Eliezer, Ioſue, Melchias, Semeias, Simeon,
\v 32 Beniamin, Maloch, Samarias.
\v 33 And of the children of Haſom, Mathanai, Mathatha, Zabad, Eliphelet, Iermai, Manaſſe, Semei.
\v 34 Of the children of Bani, Maaddi, Amram, and Vel,
\v 35 Baneas, and Badaias, Cheliau,
\v 36 Vania, Marimuth, and Eliaſib,
\v 37 Mathanias, Mathanai, and Iaſi,
\v 38 and Bani, and Bennui, Semei,
\v 39 and Salmias, and Nathan, and Adaias,
\v 40 and Mechnedebai, Siſai, Sarai,
\v 41 Ezrel, and Selemiau, Semeria,
\v 42 Sellum, Amaria, Ioſeph.
\v 43 Of the children of Nebo, Iehiel, Mathathias, Zabad, Zabina, Ieddu, and Ioel. and Banaia.
\v 44 Al theſe had taken ſtrangers to wife, and there were of them that had borne \f c \fr 10.44 \ft Amongſt other inconueniences of vnlawful mariages one is that children are borne illegitimate.\f* children.
